1
00:00:05,401 --> 00:00:08,471
NARRATOR: Summer. 2015.

2
00:00:08,572 --> 00:00:12,642
Shark attacks in the United States have reached their limit.

3
00:00:12,743 --> 00:00:14,577
911: What did you say? A shark?

4
00:00:14,677 --> 00:00:16,812
WOMAN: She ripped a man's arm off!

5
00:00:16,913 --> 00:00:21,484
NARRATOR: Just in the course of one summer,

6
00:00:21,584 --> 00:00:25,388
Sharks off the coast of the Carolinas attacked

7
00:00:25,488 --> 00:00:29,325
unexpectedly to 11 people.

8
00:00:29,425 --> 00:00:30,994
WOMAN: True. MAN: Tore off his arm.

9
00:00:32,595 --> 00:00:34,897
NARRATOR: While the attacks were making headlines.

10
00:00:34,998 --> 00:00:38,068
WOMAN: You don't see anyone swimming these days!

11
00:00:38,168 --> 00:00:43,640
NARRATOR: Experts are wondering: What's causing this sudden outbreak?

12
00:00:43,740 --> 00:00:48,145
GREG: There must be something that triggers it.

13
00:00:48,245 --> 00:00:52,649
NARRATOR: Now, two scientists from totally different worlds

14
00:00:52,749 --> 00:00:55,918
They look for the answer.

15
00:00:56,019 --> 00:01:00,423
They dive into waters full of sharks.

16
00:01:00,523 --> 00:01:03,426
JOE: You guys are completely crazy!

17
00:01:03,526 --> 00:01:07,697
NARRATOR: To test his revolutionary theory.

18
00:01:07,797 --> 00:01:10,967
GREG: He's coming so fast! He's fast!

19
00:01:11,067 --> 00:01:14,905
NARRATOR: Such attacks could be weather-related.

20
00:01:19,108 --> 00:01:24,413
If true, it could change our knowledge about sharks and rides.

21
00:01:24,513 --> 00:01:27,184
on the beach, forever.

22
00:01:36,692 --> 00:01:37,994
The Bahamas.

23
00:01:40,197 --> 00:01:43,532
Join a veteran shark scientist.

24
00:01:43,633 --> 00:01:46,502
GREG: If we can use the weather to indicate what sharks do,

25
00:01:46,602 --> 00:01:49,305
We could predict whether a shark attack will happen or not!

26
00:01:49,405 --> 00:01:52,075
And that would be wonderful!

27
00:01:52,175 --> 00:01:55,078
NARRATOR: And a very experienced meteorologist.

28
00:01:55,178 --> 00:01:59,616
JOE: I'm excited to work with Greg and show him that my hypothesis is correct:

29
00:01:59,716 --> 00:02:05,355
that wind and weather have an effect on where sharks are located.

30
00:02:06,456 --> 00:02:09,526
NARRATOR: And you might discover something very important.

31
00:02:12,995 --> 00:02:17,900
It may sound crazy that the weather can help predict the likelihood of an attack.

32
00:02:18,000 --> 00:02:23,807
of shark, but the science behind the theory is real.

33
00:02:25,641 --> 00:02:29,512
JOE: My name is Joe Merchant, I'm a meteorologist with the National Weather Service.

34
00:02:29,613 --> 00:02:32,448
My goal is to protect lives and property.

35
00:02:32,548 --> 00:02:37,687
I am trained to deal with storm warnings and hurricane watch,

36
00:02:37,787 --> 00:02:40,389
hurricane warnings, including tornado warnings.

37
00:02:40,490 --> 00:02:44,260
I've actually had to chase tornadoes.

38
00:02:44,360 --> 00:02:47,930
Look at that!

39
00:02:48,030 --> 00:02:51,200
NARRATOR: Sharks weren't part of Joe's forecast.

40
00:02:51,300 --> 00:02:56,406
until the tsunami of attacks in 2015 caught their attention.

41
00:02:58,942 --> 00:03:02,879
JOE: There were 11 shark attacks off the coast of the Carolinas.

42
00:03:02,979 --> 00:03:05,214
There I began to observe the weather.

43
00:03:05,314 --> 00:03:13,089
I observed the weather during these attacks and saw that there was a pattern.

44
00:03:13,189 --> 00:03:18,094
I decided to call the only shark expert I had read about: Dr. Skomal.

45
00:03:18,194 --> 00:03:19,996
GREG: My name is Greg Skomal, I'm a biologist.

46
00:03:20,096 --> 00:03:23,199
And affiliated with the Massachusetts Division of Marine Fisheries.

47
00:03:23,299 --> 00:03:26,068
I have studied sharks for over 30 years.

48
00:03:26,168 --> 00:03:29,939
I have focused especially on the great white shark.

49
00:03:30,039 --> 00:03:32,208
When Joes called me I had all these ideas about

50
00:03:32,308 --> 00:03:35,044
how climate change patterns might influence

51
00:03:35,145 --> 00:03:37,347
to an animal that lives in the water.

52
00:03:37,447 --> 00:03:40,750
It started to make a little sense.

53
00:03:40,850 --> 00:03:47,857
NARRATOR: Now, Joe teams up with Greg to test his bold new theory.

54
00:03:47,957 --> 00:03:54,096
Joe believes that the winds attract small marine life closer to the shore.

55
00:03:54,196 --> 00:03:59,635
These small prey attract large predators.

56
00:03:59,735 --> 00:04:05,108
Which in turn attracts the largest predators: sharks.

57
00:04:05,208 --> 00:04:08,144
Follow this food chain that the wind brings,

58
00:04:08,244 --> 00:04:14,483
This is what drives sharks towards the shore, close to swimmers.

59
00:04:14,583 --> 00:04:18,054
JOE: Taking what we know from the wind forecast,

60
00:04:18,154 --> 00:04:21,624
We can use this information and put it into a model

61
00:04:21,724 --> 00:04:26,729
and thus predict where a shark attack might occur.

62
00:04:26,829 --> 00:04:32,368
So that on a Sunday afternoon, if a family goes to the beach,

63
00:04:32,468 --> 00:04:38,474
can have a very clear idea of where the sharks are.

64
00:04:38,575 --> 00:04:43,446
NARRATOR: To test Joe's theory they want to observe two different species,

65
00:04:43,546 --> 00:04:50,619
frequently involved in human attacks: tiger sharks, and bull sharks.

66
00:04:50,720 --> 00:04:54,891
There are many of them here in the Bahamas.

67
00:04:54,991 --> 00:04:56,960
First, the tigers.

68
00:04:57,060 --> 00:04:58,427
GREG: Here we go!

69
00:04:58,527 --> 00:05:01,230
Joe, we'll head towards Tiger Beach, which is up here. JOE: Okay.

70
00:05:01,330 --> 00:05:03,132
GREG: Yeah, right at the edge of this bench.

71
00:05:03,232 --> 00:05:07,503
Uh, very deep water to the west, shallow water to the east,

72
00:05:07,603 --> 00:05:12,975
and there are lots of tiger sharks here.

73
00:05:13,075 --> 00:05:17,180
NARRATOR: Their mission: to tag sharks with tracking devices.

74
00:05:17,280 --> 00:05:22,919
to observe whether they respond to the changing climate.

75
00:05:23,019 --> 00:05:25,322
But it won't be easy.

76
00:05:28,958 --> 00:05:34,631
Tiger sharks are some of the most formidable hunters in the ocean.

77
00:05:34,731 --> 00:05:38,101
Predators with large, flat noses that can measure up to

78
00:05:38,201 --> 00:05:42,305
more than 5 meters and weigh almost a ton.

79
00:05:45,008 --> 00:05:49,178
Tiger sharks are named for their dark vertical lines.

80
00:05:49,278 --> 00:05:55,251
And they project their colors across the tropical and subtropical waters of the world.

81
00:05:56,318 --> 00:06:01,691
They have an almost limitless diet, ranging from natural prey, such as fish,

82
00:06:01,791 --> 00:06:03,493
seals and birds.

83
00:06:05,261 --> 00:06:10,699
Even human waste, such as car license plates and tires.

84
00:06:10,799 --> 00:06:17,507
This undiscerning palate sometimes also includes people.

85
00:06:17,607 --> 00:06:22,412
Leaving them second only to great white sharks in human attacks.

86
00:06:26,115 --> 00:06:29,218
And the perfect species to test the theory

87
00:06:29,318 --> 00:06:33,689
Joe's claim that wind plays a role in attacks.

88
00:06:33,790 --> 00:06:35,391
CAPTAIN: We're here, we need to anchor here.

89
00:06:35,491 --> 00:06:36,559
WOMAN: Understood.

90
00:06:52,641 --> 00:06:54,944
JOE: I've never seen a tiger shark before.

91
00:06:55,044 --> 00:06:57,213
And this is incredible.

92
00:06:57,313 --> 00:07:00,783
I think you can see my arm bristling right now.

93
00:07:00,883 --> 00:07:03,619
I'll definitely let Greg take it from here.

94
00:07:03,719 --> 00:07:09,992
I think I'll be watching the weather on the boat.

95
00:07:10,092 --> 00:07:11,761
I don't want to let go!

96
00:07:12,629 --> 00:07:15,798
GREG: Yeah, this is a good place.

97
00:07:15,898 --> 00:07:20,403
Without sharks you can't study them, so step one, complete!

98
00:07:20,503 --> 00:07:22,638
JOE: Yes. GREG: I like it.

99
00:07:22,738 --> 00:07:27,610
NARRATOR: Step two: Greg gets into the water with the tiger sharks.

100
00:07:27,710 --> 00:07:33,116
and mark them with monitoring equipment.

101
00:07:33,216 --> 00:07:36,686
GREG: We're going to attach this camera system to the shark's fin.

102
00:07:36,786 --> 00:07:40,823
It's a device that has all kinds of instrumentation in there,

103
00:07:40,923 --> 00:07:43,393
that measures temperature and depth.

104
00:07:43,493 --> 00:07:46,729
And it also has a camera system, so we can get a look

105
00:07:46,829 --> 00:07:49,199
of the shark about what is happening underwater.

106
00:07:49,299 --> 00:07:53,636
And the other instruments on it indicate the 3 movements of the shark

107
00:07:53,736 --> 00:07:57,673
to be able to know where it is going, how fast it is moving,

108
00:07:57,773 --> 00:08:02,144
up and down in the water column, in relation to temperature and depth.

109
00:08:02,244 --> 00:08:06,115
We will be able to correlate this behavior with the weather as it changes.

110
00:08:06,215 --> 00:08:08,550
and the wind increases, we will know what the shark is doing

111
00:08:08,650 --> 00:08:10,954
in relation to climate changes.

112
00:08:11,054 --> 00:08:15,190
The key will be to slide it over the dorsal fin, right here.

113
00:08:15,291 --> 00:08:20,897
Let's pull the trigger, bang! We're going.

114
00:08:20,997 --> 00:08:23,932
It won't hurt the shark, it just holds onto the fin,

115
00:08:24,032 --> 00:08:26,001
I bet he won't even react.

116
00:08:26,101 --> 00:08:29,739
It sounds easy, but I have to get as close as I can, then align it,

117
00:08:29,839 --> 00:08:31,341
and wait for him to cooperate.

118
00:08:31,441 --> 00:08:34,843
Many things have to turn out excellent.

119
00:08:34,943 --> 00:08:39,248
NARRATOR: Tigers aren't the only predators in these waters.

120
00:08:39,349 --> 00:08:45,320
Bull and lemon sharks are never far away.

121
00:08:45,421 --> 00:08:48,558
Especially when it's mealtime.

122
00:08:49,225 --> 00:08:54,329
GRANT: We have so many sharks here that we have to try to keep them

123
00:08:54,429 --> 00:08:56,599
a little far from us.

124
00:08:57,333 --> 00:09:00,736
When you have so many sharks in the same area,

125
00:09:00,836 --> 00:09:02,405
they start competing with each other.

126
00:09:11,948 --> 00:09:15,752
NARRATOR: The team is racing against the clock to complete their mission:

127
00:09:17,219 --> 00:09:19,756
a storm is approaching the islands.

128
00:09:22,325 --> 00:09:27,296
JOE: We're starting to see a little bit of instability thanks to that big storm.

129
00:09:27,396 --> 00:09:32,068
It's a sign that things are going to change here very soon.

130
00:09:32,168 --> 00:09:37,006
NARRATOR: Joe thinks the storm will hit tomorrow afternoon.

131
00:09:37,106 --> 00:09:42,178
JOE: The faster you get in the water and tag a shark, the better.

132
00:09:42,278 --> 00:09:47,850
And we'll be able to see how the shark reacts to the storm system.

133
00:09:52,688 --> 00:09:54,723
You guys are completely crazy!

134
00:09:54,823 --> 00:09:56,793
GREG: All right, I'm going after them!

135
00:10:10,439 --> 00:10:13,676
NARRATOR: The lemon sharks arrive immediately.

136
00:10:16,479 --> 00:10:19,048
It's a shark storm.

137
00:10:44,374 --> 00:10:47,076
JOE: Okay, now I'm going to put this in.

138
00:10:50,613 --> 00:10:53,983
Testing: one, two, three.

139
00:10:54,083 --> 00:10:58,053
I wanted to check that you were okay down there.

140
00:10:58,153 --> 00:11:01,724
GREG: I'm fine, Joe.

141
00:11:01,824 --> 00:11:03,559
JOE: Tell me what you're watching?

142
00:11:07,630 --> 00:11:10,099
GREG: I see a tiger! I see a tiger!

143
00:11:14,136 --> 00:11:18,541
NARRATOR: Now, Greg needs to get close enough to tag him.

144
00:11:37,693 --> 00:11:39,629
JOE: Greg? This is Joe, can you hear me?

145
00:11:42,865 --> 00:11:44,167
I don't hear anything.

146
00:11:46,535 --> 00:11:49,038
GREG: He's coming very fast! Very fast!

147
00:11:53,242 --> 00:11:55,011
I'm having a hard time marking it.

148
00:12:02,484 --> 00:12:03,986
Let's go. Let's go.

149
00:12:08,958 --> 00:12:11,994
GREG: I see a tiger! I see a tiger!

150
00:12:14,363 --> 00:12:16,732
I'm having a hard time marking it.

151
00:12:18,100 --> 00:12:22,705
NARRATOR: Shark biologist Greg Skomal and meteorologist Joe Merchant,

152
00:12:22,805 --> 00:12:26,141
They try to test a new revolutionary theory

153
00:12:26,241 --> 00:12:29,778
which could help predict shark attacks:

154
00:12:29,878 --> 00:12:33,816
They believe that sharks react to the weather, and that certain winds can

155
00:12:33,916 --> 00:12:37,920
take sharks to hunt near the coast,

156
00:12:38,020 --> 00:12:43,292
which causes shark attacks on swimmers.

157
00:12:43,392 --> 00:12:45,861
JOE: There are many!

158
00:12:45,961 --> 00:12:49,865
GREG: We look forward to seeing how changes in the climate above the water influence

159
00:12:49,965 --> 00:12:52,201
in the behavior of sharks underwater.

160
00:12:56,104 --> 00:12:59,942
NARRATOR: Greg must first tie up a complex system of cameras and computers

161
00:13:00,042 --> 00:13:03,846
to the dorsal fin of one of these tiger sharks.

162
00:13:09,218 --> 00:13:12,722
The forecast down here is for high pressure.

163
00:13:14,123 --> 00:13:15,758
Blood pressure.

164
00:13:20,930 --> 00:13:23,099
GREG: He's got Grant's fin!

165
00:13:28,303 --> 00:13:30,306
He's got Grant's dive bag!

166
00:13:33,676 --> 00:13:36,512
Oh no, he's attacking the bait box!

167
00:13:36,612 --> 00:13:42,651
NARRATOR: Then another aggressive species approaches and takes action.

168
00:13:42,751 --> 00:13:46,556
GREG: Oh, a hammerhead shark appeared out of nowhere!

169
00:13:52,761 --> 00:13:56,532
JOE: Greg, there are a lot of sharks swimming around you!

170
00:14:00,035 --> 00:14:02,071
GREG: There's complete chaos here!

171
00:14:02,171 --> 00:14:15,318
(music)

172
00:14:15,418 --> 00:14:17,052
Damn!

173
00:14:17,152 --> 00:14:21,991
(music)

174
00:14:22,091 --> 00:14:23,960
There are too many! Let's go up!

175
00:14:34,036 --> 00:14:38,807
NARRATOR: Greg's first attempt to tag a shark was too dangerous.

176
00:14:38,907 --> 00:14:40,942
JOE: Did you make it? GREG: No. They were

177
00:14:41,043 --> 00:14:46,615
JOE: No? GREG: Damn!

178
00:14:46,715 --> 00:14:55,390
It's frustrating! We came close, three or four times.

179
00:14:55,491 --> 00:14:57,326
GRANT: That tiger shark came really close!

180
00:14:57,426 --> 00:14:59,462
As soon as that bait went down to the bottom!

181
00:15:02,565 --> 00:15:04,666
GREG: You were the most popular guy on campus!

182
00:15:04,766 --> 00:15:10,206
GRANT: Yeah, they were a little too close for my liking.

183
00:15:10,306 --> 00:15:13,275
JOE: At one point, it seemed like some of the other sharks

184
00:15:13,375 --> 00:15:14,510
they were getting in each other's way a little.

185
00:15:18,847 --> 00:15:23,119
GREG: The hammerhead shark kept getting in the way.

186
00:15:23,219 --> 00:15:25,288
I never thought there would be so many sharks.

187
00:15:29,658 --> 00:15:33,696
We have to get in there and try again. JOE: Okay.

188
00:15:40,202 --> 00:15:43,806
I hope we can tag the shark very quickly.

189
00:15:43,906 --> 00:15:46,542
because that great storm is approaching,

190
00:15:46,642 --> 00:15:52,481
These are the conditions I would have expected for a shark attack.

191
00:15:52,581 --> 00:15:56,185
NARRATOR: Joe anticipates the storm will bring winds similar to those

192
00:15:56,285 --> 00:16:02,924
that occurred during the spate of attacks in the Carolinas in 2015.

193
00:16:03,024 --> 00:16:05,528
JOE: Greg, they're coming in!

194
00:16:15,671 --> 00:16:18,040
GREG: The tiger is coming!

195
00:16:25,214 --> 00:16:26,949
I'll try to mark it!

196
00:16:32,220 --> 00:16:34,390
Come here, come here!

197
00:16:43,632 --> 00:16:47,870
I got it! I did it! Finally! Yuhuuu.

198
00:17:01,250 --> 00:17:04,253
JOE: They did it! They did it! Good!

199
00:17:05,487 --> 00:17:07,289
(laughs)

200
00:17:13,428 --> 00:17:14,964
Genial.

201
00:17:20,403 --> 00:17:22,771
GREG: Yeah, we got one! GRANT: Yeah?

202
00:17:22,871 --> 00:17:26,909
GREG: We were late. One finally came over, behaved himself.

203
00:17:27,009 --> 00:17:30,780
We tried, the clamp got stuck.

204
00:17:30,880 --> 00:17:33,916
Then we came back, tried again, bang!

205
00:17:34,016 --> 00:17:36,685
We did it, great it was great.

206
00:17:36,785 --> 00:17:39,821
JOE: Excellent! GREG: Yes.

207
00:17:39,921 --> 00:17:44,860
Now the experiment begins! It's what we wanted.

208
00:17:44,960 --> 00:17:49,198
NARRATOR: All thanks to the tiger's new gadget.

209
00:17:49,298 --> 00:17:53,735
JOE: It means we're now going to be able to combine this new information with the weather data.

210
00:17:53,835 --> 00:17:59,241
And let's hope we see exactly what I think is happening:

211
00:17:59,341 --> 00:18:02,111
that the weather may be playing a role in the events

212
00:18:02,211 --> 00:18:08,117
that lead to those shark attacks.

213
00:18:08,217 --> 00:18:12,121
GREG: Every second it's collecting dozens of data points.

214
00:18:12,221 --> 00:18:14,390
JOE: Right.

215
00:18:14,490 --> 00:18:18,727
GREG: So the more seconds you're out, minutes, hours,

216
00:18:18,827 --> 00:18:20,362
we will get more information.

217
00:18:23,465 --> 00:18:25,200
But we have to get it back!

218
00:18:25,300 --> 00:18:26,802
That's the key!

219
00:18:37,179 --> 00:18:40,349
Having tagged the shark is the first step.

220
00:18:40,449 --> 00:18:43,518
Collecting data and literally collecting

221
00:18:43,618 --> 00:18:46,421
thousands of information points every minute,

222
00:18:46,522 --> 00:18:51,327
We will get a lot of this research, but only if we recover it.

223
00:18:52,728 --> 00:18:56,398
These are sharks capable of swimming hundreds of kilometers.

224
00:18:56,498 --> 00:18:59,701
And it is also a very big ocean.

225
00:18:59,801 --> 00:19:01,837
NARRATOR: The tiger shark's apparatus will take off

226
00:19:01,937 --> 00:19:06,174
in about 5 hours and will float to the surface.

227
00:19:06,274 --> 00:19:12,647
This is when the device's light will activate, allowing the team to track it.

228
00:19:12,747 --> 00:19:15,784
GREG: It could be 3 meters away or 32 kilometers away.

229
00:19:15,884 --> 00:19:20,422
So we need to make sure that all the technology takes us to the right place.

230
00:19:20,522 --> 00:19:23,392
We have to locate it using satellite receivers

231
00:19:23,492 --> 00:19:24,793
and high frequency.

232
00:19:24,893 --> 00:19:29,964
But there is always the possibility of never finding it.

233
00:19:30,065 --> 00:19:33,569
NARRATOR: Especially with the storm that's about to hit Tiger Beach soon.

234
00:19:44,179 --> 00:19:46,848
GREG: The longer he stays with the fish, the more information we'll have.

235
00:19:46,948 --> 00:19:48,350
It's very exciting.

236
00:19:48,450 --> 00:19:50,786
We have the shark swimming in the sea,

237
00:19:50,886 --> 00:19:54,122
He has the data collector and camera on his back!

238
00:19:54,223 --> 00:19:57,425
I think we should come here every hour, during the night.

239
00:19:57,525 --> 00:20:03,832
It should emerge in 4 or 5 hours, so we have time, but I want to see it.

240
00:20:08,537 --> 00:20:12,140
NARRATOR: The new dawn shows no sign of the device,

241
00:20:12,240 --> 00:20:15,243
but reveals a growing threat.

242
00:20:15,343 --> 00:20:18,313
JOE: It's a funnel cloud. That one over there is.

243
00:20:18,413 --> 00:20:22,017
It's a sign that things are changing now.

244
00:20:22,117 --> 00:20:26,755
NARRATOR: The storm Joe predicted is heading straight for them.

245
00:20:26,855 --> 00:20:30,725
JOE: You can see all those clouds gathering over there.

246
00:20:30,825 --> 00:20:32,494
GREG: It's raining a lot! JOE: Yes.

247
00:20:32,594 --> 00:20:36,365
So this is the beginning of the end of our time on the boat.

248
00:20:37,866 --> 00:20:39,301
GREG: That's very disturbing!

249
00:20:39,401 --> 00:20:41,837
(laughs)

250
00:20:41,937 --> 00:20:47,075
GREG: He knows something I don't, it's good to have a meteorologist on board.

251
00:20:47,175 --> 00:20:52,514
NARRATOR: But that big storm is also a great opportunity.

252
00:20:52,614 --> 00:20:56,351
JOE: We'll be able to see how that shark responds.

253
00:20:56,451 --> 00:21:00,122
in the face of changing weather conditions.

254
00:21:00,222 --> 00:21:02,724
NARRATOR: Now, they just need to find that device

255
00:21:02,824 --> 00:21:04,960
as soon as it comes off!

256
00:21:08,630 --> 00:21:11,100
Finally, a sign.

257
00:21:11,200 --> 00:21:13,201
GREG: Do you have it?

258
00:21:13,301 --> 00:21:14,602
ANNABELLE: Asi es.

259
00:21:14,703 --> 00:21:16,305
NARRATOR: The device emerged.

260
00:21:18,473 --> 00:21:22,011
GREG: We're close to this thing!

261
00:21:22,111 --> 00:21:25,046
Stop! There it is! I've got it! I see it!

262
00:21:25,147 --> 00:21:27,516
At 1 o'clock, arriving at 2! ANNABELLE: Yes! Of course.

263
00:21:27,616 --> 00:21:29,585
GREG: 'Now it's 3 o'clock!

264
00:21:29,685 --> 00:21:30,852
JOE: I see it too! ANNABELLE: There.

265
00:21:30,952 --> 00:21:32,321
GREG: Perfect!

266
00:21:32,421 --> 00:21:34,523
Good work, Annabelle.

267
00:21:37,726 --> 00:21:40,996
NARRATOR: But Greg isn't the only one behind the device.

268
00:21:42,097 --> 00:21:44,766
JOE: Is that a tiger? GREG: Yeah.

269
00:21:44,866 --> 00:21:46,602
Well, I'll go for it!

270
00:22:23,105 --> 00:22:24,640
JOE: Amazing!

271
00:22:32,581 --> 00:22:34,916
GREG: I got it. JOE: You're crazy.

272
00:22:35,016 --> 00:22:38,587
NARRATOR: Now, you just have to go back.

273
00:22:56,137 --> 00:22:57,272
JOE: Well done!

274
00:22:57,372 --> 00:22:58,940
GREG: I got it back.

275
00:22:59,040 --> 00:23:00,342
JOE: I would have let him win.

276
00:23:00,442 --> 00:23:04,445
I would have been more than happy to let that shark win.

277
00:23:04,545 --> 00:23:08,784
GREG: This experiment is useless if we don't get it back, it's very important!

278
00:23:08,884 --> 00:23:11,086
We're already halfway there. Let's see what's in it!

279
00:23:11,186 --> 00:23:13,989
It's like opening Christmas presents!

280
00:23:15,157 --> 00:23:18,059
JOE: I'm really excited to review the information.

281
00:23:18,159 --> 00:23:23,865
But it's time to go because there's a big storm coming in a few hours.

282
00:23:23,965 --> 00:23:26,835
And it's better for us to go.

283
00:23:29,771 --> 00:23:31,940
Tiger Beach NARRATOR: They return to the lab

284
00:23:32,040 --> 00:23:35,944
in Eleuthera to download the information

285
00:23:36,044 --> 00:23:40,949
and see if the shark reacted to the changing winds.

286
00:23:41,049 --> 00:23:42,718
GREG: Let's take a look.

287
00:23:48,423 --> 00:23:53,228
JOE: Amazing. GREG: Now you're the one! The shark!

288
00:23:53,328 --> 00:23:56,764
JOE: I try, I really do. Oh!

289
00:23:56,865 --> 00:23:58,667
GREG: There's another tiger shark! JOE: Wow.

290
00:23:58,767 --> 00:24:00,536
GREG: Isn't that great? JOE: No way! Wow!

291
00:24:00,636 --> 00:24:03,171
GREG: He's following another tiger.

292
00:24:03,271 --> 00:24:05,974
Very close up. It's wonderful!

293
00:24:06,074 --> 00:24:09,844
JOE: This footage is amazing because now I can see what the shark

294
00:24:09,944 --> 00:24:13,415
does underwater and how it behaves naturally.

295
00:24:13,515 --> 00:24:14,549
GREG: He's just swimming.

296
00:24:14,650 --> 00:24:16,752
It is now halfway down the water column.

297
00:24:18,853 --> 00:24:22,290
NARRATOR: Joe records his information to see if there is a correlation

298
00:24:22,390 --> 00:24:26,762
between climate change and shark behavior.

299
00:24:26,862 --> 00:24:28,596
And of course there is.

300
00:24:28,696 --> 00:24:33,735
GREG: Here we have a dramatic change in behavior.

301
00:24:33,835 --> 00:24:35,270
He dives twice.

302
00:24:39,641 --> 00:24:42,544
JOE: So this was, it was right before the big storm

303
00:24:42,644 --> 00:24:44,246
that made us leave tiger beach.

304
00:24:46,881 --> 00:24:53,321
GREG: They retreat before the storm hits, so they sense something.

305
00:24:53,421 --> 00:24:58,960
NARRATOR: Apparently sharks react to the change in climate.

306
00:24:59,060 --> 00:25:02,297
Now, it's time to find out if the right conditions

307
00:25:02,397 --> 00:25:05,867
of the wind will bring them closer to the coast.

308
00:25:05,967 --> 00:25:11,639
For that, they will tag a bull shark.

309
00:25:11,739 --> 00:25:15,744
GREG: We want to really see how much these coastal winds influence the behavior

310
00:25:15,844 --> 00:25:17,479
of sharks near land.

311
00:25:20,515 --> 00:25:24,052
and the species we will do it with will be bull sharks.

312
00:25:24,152 --> 00:25:29,757
It is another shark that has been involved in unprovoked attacks on people.

313
00:25:29,858 --> 00:25:33,228
It is considered one of the most dangerous sharks on the planet.

314
00:25:36,965 --> 00:25:41,002
NARRATOR: Bull sharks are named for their short, flat snout,

315
00:25:41,103 --> 00:25:50,111
and for their aggressive tendencies: they often headbutt their prey before attacking it.

316
00:25:50,211 --> 00:25:55,483
Bull sharks do not grow as large as tiger sharks, but they can reach a length of more than

317
00:25:55,583 --> 00:26:01,923
3 meters and weighing up to 200 kilograms are formidable predators that will eat

318
00:26:02,023 --> 00:26:11,800
virtually anything they see, including crabs, turtles and other sharks.

319
00:26:11,900 --> 00:26:17,672
Which could explain the extraordinary strength of its bite, kilo for kilo,

320
00:26:17,772 --> 00:26:20,642
the strongest of all the sharks in the sea.

321
00:26:25,046 --> 00:26:29,518
They often enter shallow waters, where they find people.

322
00:26:34,789 --> 00:26:38,259
Greg and Joe think the bulls may be chasing minnows.

323
00:26:38,360 --> 00:26:42,931
that approach the coast under certain wind conditions.

324
00:26:43,031 --> 00:26:47,035
JOE: Hopefully, we'll find some kind of change in the shark's behavior.

325
00:26:47,135 --> 00:26:48,036
in relation to the wind.

326
00:26:51,839 --> 00:26:57,312
NARRATOR: Greg has a lead on a bull shark hotspot:

327
00:26:57,412 --> 00:26:59,514
a nearby marina.

328
00:27:00,648 --> 00:27:02,850
GREG: I'm told bull sharks come here daily,

329
00:27:02,951 --> 00:27:07,756
so I hope I can tag one of these underwater animals.

330
00:27:07,856 --> 00:27:14,062
NARRATOR: In this murky place, Greg will put his life in danger.

331
00:27:14,162 --> 00:27:18,066
GREG: Bull sharks are very effective hunters and usually hunt

332
00:27:18,166 --> 00:27:24,773
in areas with poor visibility, and that's exactly what we have here.

333
00:27:24,873 --> 00:27:26,574
These conditions are not optimal.

334
00:27:26,674 --> 00:27:28,543
As you can see, the water is cloudy. JOE: True.

335
00:27:28,643 --> 00:27:31,045
GREG: It's even worse when you get in the water. JOE: Yeah.

336
00:27:31,145 --> 00:27:34,715
GREG: My visibility will be very limited and sharks come from any direction.

337
00:27:34,815 --> 00:27:37,519
direction, so I need you to be my eyes, okay?

338
00:27:37,619 --> 00:27:41,689
JOE: Of course.

339
00:27:41,790 --> 00:27:46,427
GREG: I have to admit I'm worried about getting in the water with bull sharks.

340
00:27:46,527 --> 00:27:49,197
I'll definitely keep my eyes open.

341
00:28:01,076 --> 00:28:03,679
JOE: Tell me what you see?

342
00:28:06,948 --> 00:28:08,116
GREG: Damn.

343
00:28:16,791 --> 00:28:20,061
JOE: Greg is in the water, and I'm actually here for safety,

344
00:28:20,161 --> 00:28:22,864
to be in communication with him.

345
00:28:22,964 --> 00:28:26,801
Staying alert and hoping we see a bull shark.

346
00:28:26,901 --> 00:28:30,538
We try to tag a bull shark, and if we succeed,

347
00:28:30,638 --> 00:28:33,508
we will be able to obtain the information we need.

348
00:28:33,608 --> 00:28:36,877
NARRATOR: Information that may prove Joe's theory, that under certain

349
00:28:36,978 --> 00:28:41,082
coastal wind conditions, like those we had today,

350
00:28:41,182 --> 00:28:44,552
could affect the behavior of sharks,

351
00:28:44,652 --> 00:28:48,390
bringing them closer to the coast and closer to us.

352
00:28:50,859 --> 00:28:55,063
Right now, Greg is in bull territory.

353
00:29:05,006 --> 00:29:06,975
JOE: Greg, it looks pretty bad from this spot.

354
00:29:07,075 --> 00:29:10,846
Can you see anything?

355
00:29:10,946 --> 00:29:13,315
GREG: Damn, I can't see anything down here!

356
00:29:19,987 --> 00:29:23,625
Wait! What's that? Is that a bull shark coming toward me?

357
00:29:25,426 --> 00:29:27,262
I'm going out. I'm going out.

358
00:29:48,082 --> 00:29:49,684
This isn't working.

359
00:29:49,784 --> 00:29:51,886
The visibility in this place is horrible.

360
00:29:51,986 --> 00:29:53,321
We'll have to do something different.

361
00:29:53,421 --> 00:29:55,557
If there was a shark there, not only would I not be able to see it,

362
00:29:55,657 --> 00:29:57,558
but there would be no way to mark it.

363
00:29:57,659 --> 00:30:01,195
And I'm not comfortable being with sharks.

364
00:30:01,295 --> 00:30:03,063
in places where I don't see them!

365
00:30:03,164 --> 00:30:08,770
I see the statistics, and I know, I'm sure that they are very good in terms of hunting,

366
00:30:08,870 --> 00:30:12,573
and catch other species in shallow, murky waters,

367
00:30:12,673 --> 00:30:14,775
and that's what we have here.

368
00:30:14,875 --> 00:30:19,980
We'll do plan B: try to tag them from the dock.

369
00:30:20,081 --> 00:30:26,721
The best way to do it is to throw fish, blood, honestly, parts into the water,

370
00:30:26,821 --> 00:30:29,891
and start attracting them with lots of bait.

371
00:30:29,991 --> 00:30:33,528
And once we have the bull shark's attention, lure them here,

372
00:30:33,628 --> 00:30:36,230
and thus mark them.

373
00:30:36,331 --> 00:30:40,602
JOE: I'd rather go looking for sharks now than watch you cut that thing.

374
00:30:40,702 --> 00:30:42,637
GREG: have you heard?

375
00:30:42,737 --> 00:30:46,240
These weathermen, huh? Come on!

376
00:30:46,340 --> 00:30:48,677
JOE: I'm a meteorologist, Greg!

377
00:30:50,645 --> 00:30:52,580
GREG: So we're hoping to attract a shark. JOE: Yeah.

378
00:30:52,680 --> 00:30:56,050
GREG: And when we do, we might have to hook him.

379
00:30:56,150 --> 00:31:01,055
JOE: I expect the winds to shift a little more to the south today.

380
00:31:01,155 --> 00:31:07,161
So I think our chances are increasing to score one of them.

381
00:31:07,261 --> 00:31:15,703
NARRATOR: The storm is out at sea, but its effects are still being felt.

382
00:31:15,804 --> 00:31:20,408
JOE: The anemometer is in use, collecting data.

383
00:31:20,508 --> 00:31:24,846
GREG: He's collecting too much, with the wind the way it is.

384
00:31:24,946 --> 00:31:28,483
JOE: Considering what we know about wind forecasts,

385
00:31:28,583 --> 00:31:33,721
We can take this information and put it into a model and thus predict

386
00:31:33,822 --> 00:31:37,892
where a shark attack could occur.

387
00:31:37,992 --> 00:31:42,029
And I think these forecasts can be very valuable for beach communities,

388
00:31:42,130 --> 00:31:50,004
especially where people are using the water recreationally.

389
00:31:50,105 --> 00:31:52,874
GREG: Joe is basically saying that atmospheric conditions

390
00:31:52,974 --> 00:31:55,510
will direct what happens beneath the surface.

391
00:31:55,610 --> 00:32:00,848
And of course I believe him: the atmosphere and water are deeply connected.

392
00:32:00,948 --> 00:32:02,483
We have a lot of activity here.

393
00:32:02,583 --> 00:32:06,121
We have a lot of remoras, which are usually attached to sharks,

394
00:32:06,221 --> 00:32:10,325
so I hope the sharks are close and these remoras just

395
00:32:10,425 --> 00:32:14,261
take off and take advantage of the bait we threw into the water.

396
00:32:14,361 --> 00:32:18,533
Come on, sharks, where are you?

397
00:32:18,633 --> 00:32:22,504
NARRATOR: Suddenly, a lemon shark.

398
00:32:22,604 --> 00:32:24,205
GREG: Did you see it?

399
00:32:24,305 --> 00:32:27,341
NARRATOR: As the sun sets, Greg takes the opportunity to score.

400
00:32:27,441 --> 00:32:30,912
to a different shallow-water shark.

401
00:32:31,012 --> 00:32:35,182
GREG: Another very common species around Eleuthera are lemon sharks.

402
00:32:35,283 --> 00:32:39,787
It is a coastal species, of shallow waters.

403
00:32:39,887 --> 00:32:43,858
And if there is one fish that is going to be affected in any way by climate changes,

404
00:32:43,958 --> 00:32:48,096
I suspect it's going to be one that lives in very shallow water.

405
00:32:51,365 --> 00:32:52,600
Is that the one over there? GRANT: Yes.

406
00:32:52,700 --> 00:32:54,736
GREG: All right! That's it!

407
00:32:54,836 --> 00:32:56,103
There's the lemon!

408
00:32:56,203 --> 00:32:59,273
Maybe we'll try to tag this lemon shark.

409
00:32:59,373 --> 00:33:00,709
JOE: Yes, it is veils!

410
00:33:03,845 --> 00:33:07,448
GREG: We have a lot of bait on that line and what the guys do

411
00:33:07,548 --> 00:33:10,318
is trying to attract that lemon closer.

412
00:33:10,418 --> 00:33:14,955
NARRATOR: They tie the end of the rope to a bright orange buoy.

413
00:33:15,055 --> 00:33:20,127
If the lemon shark bites the bait, it will pull the buoy with it,

414
00:33:20,227 --> 00:33:23,397
and will allow the team to easily locate the shark,

415
00:33:23,497 --> 00:33:27,369
tire him out and then mark him.

416
00:33:29,937 --> 00:33:31,239
GREG: There's something there! JOE: Yes.

417
00:33:31,339 --> 00:33:32,740
GREG: It could be him!

418
00:33:32,840 --> 00:33:34,142
There it is! JOE: Oh!

419
00:33:34,242 --> 00:33:36,544
GREG: There it is!

420
00:33:38,279 --> 00:33:40,882
JOE: Left! Yes! GREG: Find it!

421
00:33:40,982 --> 00:33:43,584
Be the ball, Danny!

422
00:33:43,684 --> 00:33:46,054
JOE: Come on, come on!

423
00:33:46,154 --> 00:33:47,288
-Do you have it? WOMAN: Yes.

424
00:33:49,190 --> 00:33:50,925
GREG: Watch out! JOE: Hook him up! Good!

425
00:33:53,561 --> 00:33:55,596
GREG: Watch out!

426
00:33:55,696 --> 00:33:59,134
Watch the ball. Don't stop watching the ball.

427
00:34:00,634 --> 00:34:03,003
NARRATOR: The shark takes the buoy.

428
00:34:03,104 --> 00:34:03,938
GREG: Good. JOE: Brendan!

429
00:34:06,273 --> 00:34:07,008
Curse!

430
00:34:09,577 --> 00:34:11,479
I can't believe it! GREG: What did I tell you, man?

431
00:34:11,579 --> 00:34:14,416
Bottom of the ninth, 2 cars down, we're losing by a run.

432
00:34:19,821 --> 00:34:22,890
We've got the lemon shark in that ball over there,

433
00:34:22,990 --> 00:34:26,961
He took one of the baits and we hoped to tire him out.

434
00:34:27,061 --> 00:34:30,698
When he gets tired, we'll bring him to the side of the boat, and tag him.

435
00:34:30,798 --> 00:34:31,833
But we have to move!

436
00:34:36,737 --> 00:34:39,207
That's it, ready. Here we go! We're going to do well!

437
00:34:39,307 --> 00:34:40,241
Do you have it?

438
00:34:42,076 --> 00:34:44,745
Yes, we see it. Okay, who's driving?

439
00:34:44,845 --> 00:34:48,916
NARRATOR: After days of trying to tag a shallow-water shark,

440
00:34:49,016 --> 00:34:53,053
The team's perseverance finally pays off.

441
00:34:53,153 --> 00:34:58,359
GREG: We have a lemon shark here that we've already hooked and we're going to tag.

442
00:34:58,459 --> 00:35:03,464
JOE: When they hooked the lemon, it had a little buoy that started pulling.

443
00:35:03,564 --> 00:35:06,567
It's like in the movie "Jaws."

444
00:35:06,667 --> 00:35:10,171
NARRATOR: It's the crucial first step in testing Joe's theory.

445
00:35:10,271 --> 00:35:15,376
That winds, like tonight's, affect the shark's behavior,

446
00:35:15,476 --> 00:35:18,245
taking it closer to the coast to hunt.

447
00:35:18,345 --> 00:35:20,415
And closer to us.

448
00:35:21,916 --> 00:35:26,954
JOE: We have a strong front that's going to be moving northwest.

449
00:35:27,054 --> 00:35:30,291
So. If we can tag the shark once and for all,

450
00:35:30,391 --> 00:35:33,294
we will be able to see its behavior as the front approaches.

451
00:35:34,829 --> 00:35:36,798
My skin is goosebumps

452
00:35:36,898 --> 00:35:38,900
This is amazing!

453
00:35:39,767 --> 00:35:42,336
It's heading to

454
00:35:42,436 --> 00:35:45,773
goes to the entrance!

455
00:35:45,873 --> 00:35:48,509
GREG: No way! I don't like him running.

456
00:35:48,609 --> 00:35:49,577
DUNCAN: Around those piles!

457
00:35:49,677 --> 00:35:51,312
GREG: Damn! That's bad!

458
00:35:52,413 --> 00:35:54,148
NARRATOR: The slippery lemon moves

459
00:35:54,248 --> 00:35:57,785
through the maze of boats in the place.

460
00:35:57,885 --> 00:36:03,124
You put yourself at risk by hooking your buoy around a pile or propeller.

461
00:36:03,224 --> 00:36:05,793
GREG: Right under the boat. GRANT: It's behind the boat.

462
00:36:05,893 --> 00:36:10,765
GREG: Damn! Looks like, I think it's stuck there.

463
00:36:10,865 --> 00:36:12,566
GRANT: Neutral! Let him go. Let him go.

464
00:36:12,666 --> 00:36:16,003
GREG: Don't stop him. Good.

465
00:36:16,103 --> 00:36:18,706
DUNCAN: Remember they can bite their own tails!

466
00:36:18,806 --> 00:36:20,241
GREG: Yes.

467
00:36:20,808 --> 00:36:22,810
GRANT: Come on. Neutral! GREG: Neutral.

468
00:36:25,913 --> 00:36:28,216
Got it! Got it, here we go.

469
00:36:29,517 --> 00:36:33,721
NARRATOR: But just when he's about to reach it.

470
00:36:33,821 --> 00:36:35,156
BRENDAN: Oh, I can't believe it.

471
00:36:38,125 --> 00:36:40,361
GREG: He spit it out! Look at that!

472
00:36:40,461 --> 00:36:43,631
He had everything inside, he had everything in his mouth.

473
00:36:43,731 --> 00:36:45,800
He bit it all! Wow!

474
00:36:45,900 --> 00:36:50,371
Damn! It didn't catch.

475
00:36:53,307 --> 00:36:55,910
JOE: Why are they coming back?

476
00:37:00,248 --> 00:37:03,718
It can't be! What happened?

477
00:37:04,785 --> 00:37:07,689
GREG: That fish got off the hook. Bye.

478
00:37:07,789 --> 00:37:10,391
He left without the device.

479
00:37:10,491 --> 00:37:15,897
We had a lemon shark hooked and it dragged the ball all over the harbor,

480
00:37:15,997 --> 00:37:18,566
so it looked like it was pretty hooked

481
00:37:18,666 --> 00:37:21,902
and we were ready to tag this fish

482
00:37:22,003 --> 00:37:24,972
and we brought it closer to the boat and the hook came out.

483
00:37:25,072 --> 00:37:31,079
Bang! It's over. Goodbye.

484
00:37:31,179 --> 00:37:37,451
Well, let's finish for today and we'll try again tomorrow.

485
00:37:37,551 --> 00:37:39,254
Tomorrow will be another day

